Per-query co-visitation trace for DiskANN.

DISKANN_VISIT_TRACE gives per-node visit COUNTS aggregated over the workload.
That is enough to recover H(N), but it says nothing about which nodes are
visited BY THE SAME QUERY -- and that is the quantity that bounds how few pages
a query could touch under an optimal on-disk layout.

Motivation (Knowledge/cache-benefit-ceiling): the achievable benefit of any
eagerly-populated cache is 1 - 1/(reuse*d), where d is useful nodes delivered
per page read. Measured reuse is 1.076 on SIFT100M, so with today's d=1 a
perfect cache is worth 7% of search I/O. Raising d is the only lever with real
headroom, and d is governed by co-visitation, not by per-node frequency.

Existing layout work (Starling, PageANN, BAMG, MARGO) co-locates GRAPH
NEIGHBOURS as a proxy for this. Co-visitation is the direct objective: nodes
expanded in the same query should share a page. This patch measures it so the
headroom can be computed before any layout is built.

Record format, appended once per query: [uint32 n][n x uint32 node_id].
The reader loops to EOF. Each record is written under a mutex so it is atomic;
records from different threads interleave, which is fine since each is a set.

Armed only AFTER generate_cache_list_from_sample_queries, so DiskANN's own
sample searches are excluded and only the real workload is recorded.

Applies ON TOP OF visit_trace.patch -- the arm site and destructor hooks sit
adjacent to that patch's. Build from ann-suite/diskann:visittrace.

Buffering costs a push_back per expanded node plus one locked write per query,
so this perturbs timing. Use it for layout characterization only, never for a
timed measurement.

Env-var gated by DISKANN_COVISIT_TRACE=<path>; completely inert when unset.

